# Wan2.2 5B Shot SOP

This SOP turns GPT key images and shot notes into short local video clips for Remotion. It uses only the existing Wan2.2-TI2V-5B baseline.

## Mandatory ChatGPT Web Image Repair Route

This video workflow uses ChatGPT web image generation/editing as the required
production repair route when still-image quality is the reason a video clip
fails. The Codex Desktop built-in image tool is not allowed for formal video
source images, clean plates, texture references, or final repaired keyframes.
It is forbidden for this video-making workflow, including previews, fallback,
drafts, temporary candidates, and final sources.

Formal image source rule:

```text
All images created or repaired for this video pipeline must use the logged-in
ChatGPT web official image tool. Do not call the Codex built-in image tool.
```

Use it before giving up on a shot when the failure is visible in one or more
frames:

- bad key image before generation
- object removal leaves dark patches, silhouettes, or fake texture
- plant/person/product removal fails at frame level
- background, desk mat, wall, cloth, skin, or product texture looks unnatural
- a video model take is close, but a reference frame or input image is wrong

Process:

1. Extract the failed keyframes or pick the bad key image.
2. Open/inspect the image evidence before editing.
3. Use ChatGPT web image generation/editing to repair the still image.
4. Preserve required invariants: camera angle, subject pose, foreground object,
   lighting, shadows, device/keyboard/phone geometry, and intended composition.
5. Save the repaired image into the shot folder or ComfyUI input folder.
6. Use the repaired image as a keyframe replacement, clean plate, texture
   reference, or direct frame source for the next controlled video attempt.
7. Render a new clip or repaired segment.
8. Verify with ffprobe plus contact sheet/video review.

Do not treat a single repaired still as a passed video. The video only passes
after the repaired still has been used in the video pipeline and the resulting
clip passes motion, texture, frame consistency, and artifact checks.

When using ChatGPT web image editing:

1. Upload/paste the exact failed frame into the logged-in ChatGPT web page.
2. Ask for a repaired image with explicit preserve/remove constraints.
3. Screenshot-confirm the image thumbnail is attached before sending.
4. Save the upload/send/result screenshots into the shot evidence folder.
5. Save, crop, or download the returned image into the shot evidence folder.
6. Treat the returned image as the required high-quality source if it is
   visually better than the old frame.
7. If the video assembly creates artifacts, fix the assembly method: direct
   keyframe replacement, tighter masks, alignment, or more repaired keyframes.
8. Do not reject the web-repaired still merely because one rough transfer pass
   failed.
9. If one web-repaired frame is not enough for motion, request more repaired
   keyframes; ChatGPT web can produce several needed images quickly.

Batching:

- Default to at most 10 images per ChatGPT web image batch.
- If more than 10 failed frames are needed, split into `batch_01`, `batch_02`,
  etc.
- If the ChatGPT web page shows a lower upload/generation limit, follow the
  lower visible limit.
- Save one prompt file and one evidence folder per batch.

## Inputs

For each shot, prepare:

- scene id
- shot id
- one key image, preferably square or close to final crop
- positive prompt
- negative prompt
- intended duration
- filename prefix

Put key images in:

```text
H:\AI\ComfyUI_Aaki_v3\ComfyUI\input
```

Suggested image name:

```text
scene##_shot##_key.png
```

If a ChatGPT web repaired image is created, save it with an explicit name such as:

```text
scene##_shot##_gpt_repaired_key.png
scene##_shot##_gpt_repaired_clean_plate.png
scene##_shot##_gpt_texture_reference.png
```

Record the source frame, ChatGPT web prompt, upload screenshot, generated-result
screenshot, local saved image path, and reason for repair in the shot report.

